    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1131">

                <pb facs="0939" n="823" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg823"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/4514.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Consul at Ciudad
                            Juárez to the Secretary of
                    State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Ciudad Juárez</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-07-29">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 29, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mexican consul at El Paso, who is in Chargé of Federal troops north of Casas
                    Grandes, promises to give immediate orders to crowd rebels in that section. I
                    would suggest curtailment of importations at this port to the United States, as
                    well as exports. Rebels are gathering funds from collections on imports and
                    exports, which induces them to remain; curtailment would weaken them and hasten
                    their departure. Great alarm caused by statements of subordinate rebel officers
                    of intention to force intervention. Conditions will improve with Federal
                    advance.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edwards.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
